---
title: Learning Metal with FlashAttention
date: '2026-09'
---

> <span class="note-label">🦉 Note 🦉</span>
>
> This is a follow-up to [Learning MLX](/blog/learning-mlx).

Having primarily worked with Apple hardware for the last decade (at least locally),
I've thought it might (finally) make sense learn more about Apple's Metal Shading Language (MLS). 
**Hence, in this case study, we will develop performant FlashAttention Metal implementations** and make them accessible to Python via Apple's array framework MLX.
For learning Metal, FlashAttention ([Dao et al., 2022](#ref-dao2022flashattention)) seems like an excellent choice, 
since, as in CUDA programming, it
requires a deep understanding of the memory hierarchy on Apple GPUs and how to efficiently make use of it.
Also, since MLX has its own FlashAttention implementation, we
can easily compare our implementation against theirs.

Specifically, we will first have a look at the differences between Metal and CUDA.
We then derive FlashAttention, and
try to come up with a implementation in Metal that is compute- and memory-efficient. Finally, we compare our implementation(s)
against the native implemention of FlashAttention from MLX. Since MLX exposes Python bindings, **we also show how to use a FlashAttention Metal kernel
directly within a transformer architecture in Python**.

Code can be found on GitHub at
[dirmeier/flashattention-metal](https://github.com/dirmeier/flashattention-metal).

## Metal vs CUDA

When having a first look at MSL (and Apple's naming conventions in general), it might be
confusing to understand what they mean (well, technically, the same is true for CUDA).
On the bright side, most Metal concepts have a CUDA counterpart, and the language itself
seems like a dialect of CUDA rather than a complete different take on GPGPU programming.

| Concept                  | CUDA                          | Metal                             | Has better name |
| ------------------------ | ----------------------------- | --------------------------------- | --------------- |
| lockstep thread group    | warp (32 threads)             | simdgroup (32 threads)            | Metal           |
| cooperating block        | thread block                  | threadgroup                       | Metal           |
| hardware unit            | streaming multiprocessor (SM)     | GPU core                          | neither         |
| on-chip scratch          | shared memory                 | threadgroup memory                | Metal           |
| off-chip memory          | global memory (HBM)           | device memory (unified)           | CUDA           |
| entry point              | `__global__ void`             | `kernel void`                     | CUDA           |
| read-only uniforms       | `__constant__`                | `constant` address space          | tie             |
| pointer aliasing hint    | `__restrict__`                | `__restrict__`                    | tie             |
| block index              | `blockIdx`                    | `threadgroup_position_in_grid`    | CUDA            |
| thread index in block    | `threadIdx`                   | `thread_position_in_threadgroup`  | CUDA            |
| global thread index      | computed from the two above   | `thread_position_in_grid`         | Metal           |
| lane index               | `threadIdx.x % warpSize`      | `thread_index_in_simdgroup`       | Metal           |
| warp index in block      | `threadIdx.x / warpSize`      | `simdgroup_index_in_threadgroup`  | Metal           |
| lockstep width           | `warpSize`                    | `threads_per_simdgroup`           | Metal           |
| block-wide barrier       | `__syncthreads()`             | `threadgroup_barrier()`           | CUDA           |
| simdgroup-wide barrier   | `__syncwarp()`                | `simdgroup_barrier()`             | CUDA            |
| lane exchange            | `__shfl_xor_sync()`           | `simd_shuffle_xor()`              | Metal           |
| matrix fragment          | `wmma::fragment`              | `simdgroup_float8x8`              | Metal           |
| matrix multiply-add      | `wmma::mma_sync()`            | `simdgroup_multiply_accumulate()` | Metal           |

Many of the concepts are doing similar things. For instance, 
entry points to the GPU functions, block/thread/global thread indexes, 
tensor matrix multiplication, etc. only use different names.

Also both CUDA and Metal work in groups of 32 threads that execute in lockstep.
However, it's worth noting CUDA does SIMT while Metal does SIMD. To summarize the differences:

- On an execution level both CUDA SIMT and Metal SIMD have identical issues 
for diverging branches. Both use 32 ALUs that share a single instruction dispatcher.
If the branches diverge, e.g., on 16 threads, then the other 16 threads have to wait.
- However, the advantage of SIMT is that a warp can interleave during a diverging branch, since each thread has its own program counter, which
can resolve "cross-lane spin-waits" (i.e., one set of threads waiting for the other set), while
a simdgroup cannot interleave.  
- SIMD is, I guess, more efficient since there is only one prgram counter and no call stack.
-  Ironically, every warp (SIMT) thread, can
also do SIMD in addition, since data is stored in 32bit registers. This allows for using, e.g., using 2 16bit floats in parallel
using `__h***` functions.
- So: SIMT ⊃ SIMD.

The biggest difference is probably what an SM on a CUDA device and GPU core on an Apple GPU represent.
Both have their own schedulers, registers and threadgroup memory, but 
but NVIDIA publishes SM diagrams down to ALU counts and warp-scheduler partitions, 
while Apple publishes none of that 🙂.

There's also some differences in the respective toolchain which are not really significantly better or worse 
(even though I prefer writing CUDA kernels).

| Concept           | CUDA                     | Metal                     | Has better name |
| ----------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------- | --------------- |
| launch            | `kernel<<<g, b, s, t>>>` | `dispatchThreadgroups()`  | CUDA           |
| work queue        | stream                   | command queue and buffer  | CUDA           |
| device buffer     | `cudaMalloc`             | `MTLBuffer`               | CUDA             |
| compiler          | `nvcc`                   | `xcrun metal`             | tie             |
| compiled artefact | cubin / fatbin           | `.metallib`               | Metal           |
| compiler IR       | PTX                      | AIR                       | tie         |
| disassembler      | `cuobjdump`              | `xcrun metal-objdump`     | tie             |
| vendor BLAS       | cuBLAS                   | MPS and MPSGraph          | CUDA            |


## FlashAttention

Given queries, keys and values $Q, K, V \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times D}$, standard
attention computes, for each query row $i$,

$$o_i = \sum_j \mathrm{softmax}_j(s_{ij})\, v_j, \qquad s_{ij} = \frac{q_i \cdot k_j}{\sqrt{D}},$$

where the softmax normalises over all $j$. Computing $o_i$
requires the full row $s_{i:}$ before the softmax over it can even be
computed. For all outputs $O$, we consequently need to materialize the entire $N\times N$-dimensional
 score matrix $S$.

To compute the softmax in a numerically stable manner, array frameworks substract the row maximum
before computing the expoential:

$$\mathrm{softmax}_j(s_{ij}) = \frac{\exp(s_{ij} - \max_j s_{ij})}{\sum_j \exp(s_{ij} - \max_j s_{ij})},$$

FlashAttention keeps a running maximum
instead and rescales accumulated values whenever the maximum
increases. Instead of materializing the entire score matrix $S$, FlashAttention splits the $N$ queries, keys and values
into row-tiles of a fixed size (e.g., 32 rows)
that fit into SMEM, and processes one tile at a time.

Let $m^{(t)}$ be the running maximum after tile $t$, $Z^{(t)}$ the running
softmax normalising constant, and $\tilde{O}^{(t)}$ the running, unnormalised
output matrix. For a new tile of scores $s_j$:

$$m^{(t)} = \max\left(m^{(t-1)},\ \max_j s_j\right), \qquad \alpha = \exp\left(m^{(t-1)} - m^{(t)}\right)$$

$$Z^{(t)} = \alpha Z^{(t-1)} + \sum_j \exp\left(s_j - m^{(t)}\right), \qquad \tilde{O}^{(t)} = \alpha \tilde{O}^{(t-1)} + \sum_j \exp\left(s_j - m^{(t)}\right) v_j$$

After the last tile the the output is $O = \tilde{O}^{(T)} / Z^{(T)}$. 

As pseudocode:

```text
for each Q-tile (rows of Q):
    m, Z, O~ = -inf, 0, 0
    for each K/V-tile t (also rows of K/V):
        s  = Q_tile @ K_tile^T / sqrt(D)
        m_new = max(m, max_j(s))
        alpha  = exp(m - m_new)
        Z  = alpha * Z  + sum_j(exp(s - m_new))
        O~ = alpha * O~ + exp(s - m_new) @ V_tile
        m  = m_new
    O_tile = O~ / Z
```

For our Metal Flashattention implementations, a threadgroup owns one tile of `tile_size ` query rows and loops over the key tiles. 
The grid is dispatched as (seq_len / tile_size, heads, batch), so tgid.z is the batch, tgid.y the head and tgid.x the query tile.
Hence, each of the `seq_len / tile_size` dispatched threadgroups independently 
runs the inner for-loop over all K/V-tiles to produce its own O_tile.
All three implementations below (v1–v3) share this same outer structure and only differ in what happens inside that inner loop.

## v1: everything through threadgroup memory

In a first attempt, we compute both matrix products using the simdgroup
matrix engine. In Metal `simdgroup_float8x8` fragments and
`simdgroup_multiply_accumulate` fill the same role as CUDA's tensor
core primitives (i.e., `wmma::*`): an 8×8 fragment of a matrix is computed using a single group of 32 threads.
The data (the 8x8 fragment of a matrix) that is used for this computation is stored directly in registers.

$Q$, $K, the running output $\tilde{O}$ and the score $S$ all are stored in threadgroup memory (SMEM):

```cpp
threadgroup float* scores = shared;                  // tile_size * tile_size
threadgroup float* O_tilde_smem  = scores + tile_size * tile_size; // tile_size * dim
threadgroup float* Q_smem  = O_tilde_smem + tile_elems;    // tile_size * dim
threadgroup float* K_smem  = Q_smem + tile_elems;    // tile_size * dim
```

The values $V$ are still read from device memory (DRAM), since the
32 KB of SMEM on Apple silicon would likely overflow if we stored them there, too.

When doing a matrix multiply-accumulate using `simdgroup_float8x8`, the frament (i.e., a submatrix of size 8x8), 
is loaded directly into the registers of the 32 threads that the simdgroup owns. 
For instance, the code for the matmul of the scores is shown below (find the rest of the code on [GitHub](https://github.com/dirmeier/flashattention-metal), please):

```cpp
constant constexpr uint kFrag = 8;

// load K into SMEM, tile_size rows at a time
for (uint r = 0; r < tile_size; ++r) {
    for (uint c = tid; c < dims.dim; c += threads) {
        K_smem[r * dims.dim + c] = kb[(k0 + r) * dims.dim + c];
    }
}
// make K_smem visible for all threads in threadgroup
threadgroup_barrier(mem_flags::mem_threadgroup);

// S = Q * K^T, computed entirely in registers via the matrix engine
for (uint i = 0; i < tile_size / kFrag; ++i) {
    for (uint j = 0; j < tile_size / kFrag; ++j) {
        simdgroup_float8x8 s_frag = simdgroup_float8x8(0.0f);
        for (uint d = 0; d < dims.dim; d += kFrag) {
            simdgroup_float8x8 fq;
            simdgroup_float8x8 fk;
            simdgroup_load(fq, Q_smem + i * kFrag * dims.dim + d, dims.dim);
            simdgroup_load(fk, K_smem + j * kFrag * dims.dim + d, dims.dim, ulong2(0), true);
            // S = Q * K^T matmul 
            simdgroup_multiply_accumulate(s_frag, fq, fk, s_frag);
        }
        ...
    }
}
```

Since a thread owns only 2 values (and not all 8), the 8 values of a row are split across 4 threads. 
We consequently would need to write the values of the 4 threads to SMEM 
such that we can compute the running max $m^{(t)}$ and sum over a row. We can do this by calling `simdgroup_store`
which, directly writes all 64 values (which are currently in the registers of 32 threads) to SMEM. We iterate over all
fragments of a tile (which in this/our case is a 32x32 score matrix $S$), until one tile is computed.

```cpp
for (uint i = 0; i < tile_size / kFrag; ++i) {
    for (uint j = 0; j < tile_size / kFrag; ++j) {
        ...
        // write from registers to SMEM `scores`
        simdgroup_store(s_frag, scores + i * kFrag * tile_size + j * kFrag, tile_size);
    }
}
// wait till all threads in threadgroup have finished doing `simdgroup_store`
// -> all S in SMEM visible to all threads in the threadgroup (and therefore every simdgroup within it)
threadgroup_barrier(mem_flags::mem_threadgroup);

const bool owns_row = tid < tile_size;
// only one thread should read back from SMEM to register per row
if (owns_row) {  
  for (uint j = 0; j < tile_size; ++j) {
    float s = scores[tid * tile_size + j] * dims.scale;
    // do max and sum computations
    ...
  }
}
```

## v2: keeping the scores in registers

We can gain a simple speed-up over v1 by leaving all data in the registers, i.e., not doing a `simdgroup_store`, `threadgroup_barrier`,
and writing back `float s` once per row and thread.

Recall that we used `simdgroup_multiply_accumulate` to do matrix multiplications on 8x8 fragments (of previously tiled Q, K and V tensors) 
and that a simdgroup uses 32 threads to store the 8x8=64 values.

Metal exposes the two values of each thread through `thread_elements()` meaning we have a way to access the values while they
are stored in registers. We can then use `simd_shuffle_xor` to exchance values between threads (lanes).
Given v1 of our algorithm, one should immediatly see why this is cool 🤯 🥳. 

Specifically, for each thread in a simdgroup, we first compute the max of its 2 values per fragment.
Since a simdgroup has 32 threads, this can be represented with 5 bits in binary from 00000 to 11111.
We then use `simd_shuffle_xor` to get the max when shuffling with lane 00001 (1u) and lane 01000 (8u). 
Why? Apple doesn't report which lane holds which elements of the 8x8 fragment, so one has to test empirically and hope it is correct 🙂.
A sketch of the code that v2 uses is shown below:

```cpp
// each thread reduces its own 2 values (per fragment) to one local max
// we know from before that the 2 values of a thread belong to one row of the 8x8 fragment
float block_max = -INFINITY;
for (uint j = 0; j < tile_size / kFrag; ++j) {
    thread auto& e = s_frag[j].thread_elements();
    for (uint i = 0; i < 2; ++i) {
        e[i] *= dims.scale;
        block_max = max(block_max, e[i]);
    }
}

// combine across the 4 lanes holding this row
block_max = max(block_max, simd_shuffle_xor(block_max, 1u));  // swap with lane ^1
block_max = max(block_max, simd_shuffle_xor(block_max, 8u));  // swap with lane ^8
// -> every one of the 4 lanes now holds the true max of the row
```

Consequently, the scores and the output accumulator never have to be written to SMEM,
 but all computations can stay in registers.

## v3: more optimisations

Version v3 does some more optimisations that I found in MLX's [original implementation](https://github.com/ml-explore/mlx/blob/117188cd735299f396a08f7697a81759b1e0550b/mlx/backend/metal/kernels/steel/attn/kernels/steel_attention.h#L70):

- read K directly transposed into SMEM,
- use a shared KV buffer, i.e., once QK^T is computed, write V into the same SMEM address that K was written to,
- work in log-space.

## Calling it from MLX

To use our FlashAttention Metal implementations in Python
Python, MLX offers a function called `mx.fast.metal_kernel`, which compiles a kernel body at *runtime* (yes).

Hence, Python API has to provide the template parameters that we used to define the FlashAttention implementation:

```python
KERNEL_ = mx.fast.metal_kernel(
  name="flash_attention_mlx_v3",
  input_names=["q", "k", "v"],
  output_names=["out"],
  source=(SHADERS_PATH_ / "flashattention_mlx.metal").read_text(),
  header=(SHADERS_PATH_ / "flashattention.metal").read_text(),
)

def _mlx_attention(q, k, v, causal=True):
  B, H, T, D = q.shape
  keys = 32 if D <= 64 else 16
  shared = 32 * (D + 4) + max(D * (keys + 4), keys * (D + 4))
  return KERNEL_(
    inputs=[q, k, v],
    template=[("kVersion", 3), ("kDim", D), ("kSeq", T), ("kBatch", B),
              ("kHeads", H), ("kCausal", causal), ("kTile", 32),
              ("kShared", shared), ("kKTile", keys)],
    grid=(128 * (T // 32), H, B),
    threadgroup=(128, 1, 1),
    output_shapes=[q.shape],
    output_dtypes=[q.dtype],
  )[0]
```

With this, we can, for instance, define an MLX neural network module and use our Metal shader. Here, we naturally use a
transformer block:

```python
class TransformerBlock(nn.Module):
  def __init__(self, dims: int, heads: int) -> None:
    super().__init__()
    self.heads = heads
    self.head_dim = dims // heads
    self.qkv = nn.Linear(dims, 3 * dims, bias=False)
    self.out_proj = nn.Linear(dims, dims, bias=False)
    self.mlp = nn.Sequential(
      nn.Linear(dims, 4 * dims), nn.GELU(), nn.Linear(4 * dims, dims)
    )
    self.pre_attention_rmsnorm = nn.RMSNorm(dims)
    self.pre_mlp_rmsnorm = nn.RMSNorm(dims)

  def _attention(self, x: mx.array) -> mx.array:
    B, T, D = x.shape
    q, k, v = (
      t.reshape(B, T, self.heads, self.head_dim).transpose(0, 2, 1, 3)
      for t in mx.split(self.qkv(x), 3, axis=-1)
    )
    o = _mlx_attention(q, k, v)
    return self.out_proj(o.transpose(0, 2, 1, 3).reshape(B, T, D))

  def __call__(self, x: mx.array) -> mx.array:
    x = x + self._attention(self.pre_attention_rmsnorm(x))
    x = x + self.mlp(self.pre_mlp_rmsnorm(x))
    return x
```

That's it!

## Benchmark

Let's compare the 3 implementations against MLX's FlashAttention implementation.
All numbers are computed by running on an Apple M1 and averaged over 10 iterations (with 3 warmup runs).
The (B, H, S, D)-dimensional input tensor uses [2, 4, 2048, 128] elements. The results are given below:

| kernel | ms | GFLOP/s |
| ------ | -- | ------- |
| v1 — scores through SMEM, $V$ from DRAM | 264.2 | 65.0 |
| v2 — scores and $O$ in registers | 39.7 | 432.5 |
| v3 — transposed $K$, shared $KV$ buffer | 10.6 | 1615.2 |
| MLX's own attention | **10.0** | **1712.3** |

MLX's implementation is cleary better than the ones we produced, even though with v3 we came pretty close.
Curiously, moving from v2 to v3 almost gave us a 4 times speed up.

When using different sequence lengths and head dimensions:

| S | D | v3 ms | MLX ms |
| --- | --- | ----- | ------ |
| 512  | 64  | **0.68** | 1.02 |
| 512  | 128 | **1.12** | 1.90 |
| 1024 | 64  | 1.66 | **1.39** |
| 1024 | 128 | **3.28** | 4.41 |
| 2048 | 64  | 5.45 | **4.81** |
| 2048 | 128 | 10.64 | **10.03** |

Here, v3 is faster than MLX at some of the smaller shapes. Assumingly, MLX is optimized for larger sequence lengths and head
dimensions, since they are more frequently found in larger transformer models.

## Conclusion

Learning the basics of Metal was easier than I expected. The language is very close to CUDA and, aside from naming conventions, 
even feels a bit easier to write. However, NVIDIA's documentation is significantly better than anything I've found from Apple, which
makes working with Metal somewhat painful.

Hope reading this was informative to some 🙂🦉.

## References

<span id="ref-dao2022flashattention"></span>

